NAME
    capabilities - how authority works on Horus

DESCRIPTION
    Every operation a program performs is authorised by a capability it
    holds: an unforgeable reference to one object, with a set of rights.
    A program names a capability by a slot number in its own capability
    space and never sees the capability itself, so it cannot forge one,
    guess one or read another program's.

    NOTHING IS GRANTED FOR WHO YOU ARE. There is no ambient authority: no
    privilege for being a particular user, for having been started by the
    kernel, or for asking first. A program can do exactly what its
    capabilities name, and a new program starts with only what its parent
    chose to give it.

    AUTHORITY ONLY SHRINKS. A capability can be copied (mint), or given to
    a program its holder supervises (grant), and each copy has at most the
    rights of its source. No operation adds a right.

    REVOCATION FOLLOWS THE TREE. Each capability records the one it was
    derived from, so the capabilities form a tree. Revoking one removes it
    and everything derived from it, in every program's capability space,
    and nothing else: not its source, not its siblings, not another
    capability to the same object. Each capability has a 64-bit serial,
    issued once and never again; capview(1) prints the tree.

    THE KERNEL IS SMALL ON PURPOSE. It enforces capabilities and little
    else. The filesystem, the console and init are ordinary ring-3
    programs holding the capabilities their job needs.

    These claims, each with the test or proof that checks it, are the
    table in SECURITY.md in the source tree.

